<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Albano</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Alban Lake, Lacus Albanus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Albano, the ancient Alban Lake, is a volcanic crater lake in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-hills">Alban Hills</a> south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Its shores were settled from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> and were traditionally associated with Alba Longa, which Roman writers placed nearby. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> cut a drainage tunnel through the crater rim, traditionally dated to 398–397 BCE, and the papal summer residence of Castel Gandolfo overlooks the lake today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-albano.jpg" alt="Lake Albano" width="320" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Livioandronico2013 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panoramica_Lago_Albano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake Albano, also called Lago di Castel Gandolfo, is a small volcanic crater lake in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-hills">Alban Hills</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, at the foot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cavo">Monte Cavo</a>, 20 km south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. It is about 170 m deep, the deepest lake in Lazio, and measures 3.5 km by 2.3 km. It was formed by the overlapping union of two volcanic craters, as the ridge in its centre, rising 70 m, shows. In Roman times it was known as Albanus Lacus and lay not far from the ancient city of Alba Longa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> reports that in 406 BCE the lake surged over the surrounding hills although no rain fell and no tributaries fed it. The flood destroyed fields and vineyards before pouring into the sea. It is thought to have been a limnic eruption, in which volcanic gases trapped in sediment on the lake bed built up and were suddenly released.</p><p>During the wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and Veii a discharge tunnel was cut through the crater walls to control flooding, a gigantic work given the modest means of the time, said to have been built by 30,000 men. It is 1,350 m long and runs 128 m below the surface. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> says the Oracle of Delphi prompted the work, declaring that Rome could beat Veii only when the lake's waters were channelled and used for irrigation. In 393 BCE the lake rose again without rain, and a haruspex of Veii recited a prophecy that Veii would be impregnable while the waters stayed high. Georges Dumézil saw the story as a festival myth giving significance to an exceptional event.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Castel Gandolfo, which overlooks the lake, is the site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apostolic-palace">Papal Palace</a>. The lake hosted the canoeing and rowing events of the 1960 Summer Olympic Games in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and the lane marking system developed for them is commonly called the Albano buoy system.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Albano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Archaeology shows Iron Age villages and cemeteries around the <b>Alban Lake</b> and Monte Cavo from about 1000 BCE, but no single city has been firmly identified.</blockquote><a href="/state/alba-longa">Alba Longa</a></li></ul></section>
